hello and welcome to the new world  review your source for everything anime  and manga and today I'm going to be  sharing some final thoughts on the  recently concluded season 2 of one punch  man just a very early warning this video  will contain spoilers so you know  continue on at your own risk  now one punch man season 2 is a series  that has garnered quite a lot of  unfortunate attention primarily due to  the comparison with its first season  produced by the legendary madhouse  whereas this season was produced by  j.c.staff and right at the outset I want  to say that I did not rewatch season 1  before going into season 2 because I  really didn't think that it would be  fair to bring those kind of expectations  into this experience regardless there is  no denying the specter of madhouse that  looms over the entirety of the 12  episodes presented here as one punch man  was a mega hit for the anime world so  season 2 would have had impossible shoes  to fill even if the same production  company were behind it so with all of  that in mind how to season 2 hold up as  a product of its own pretty well I'd say  it was most certainly an enjoyable 12  episodes animated fairly well and with  great vocal performances I think one  thing in particular that was simply  nailed by season two was comedy there  were very few intentional comic beats  that failed to have me laughing and it  made me realize that this world is still  so much fun to be a part of and one  thing that always gets me are the names  of the various monsters with centi kohai  and cindy senpai being some of my  absolute favorites of the season full  credit of which goes to the original  author 1 for having such an obscure yet  direct sense of humor as well as story  but the anime adaptation ran with it  rather nicely as well the highlight of  the season for me was definitely the  addition of Gaara though he was a very  slow burn like when we were first  introduced to him he seemed very bad guy  for the sake of being bad guy you know  the whole I'm not a man I'm a monster so  I'm just going to destroy things with no  good reason but that perception is  gradually dispelled and he becomes  probably the most depth fill character  in the entire season through him we  explore some of the philosophy of what  it is to be a hero and a monster the  former of which has also heavily focused  on by all of the other heroes in one  punch man but Gaara brings quite a nice  unique spin to it having experienced  heroism from the perspective of being  unjustly labelled as a monster which  goes on to become a self-fulfilling  prophecy as he somewhat adopts the idea  of this existence but Gaara especially  in his flashbacks really drew attention  to the darker side of heroics but a lot  of this work being done during the final  episode the series showcasing that the  most popular kid in school had the  innate right to play the hero whilst he  as an outcast was relegated to the  villain and not only that but instead of  playing fairly the hero would bring down  the villain through unbalanced means in  Cara's case by being held down by  the kids but this was also nicely  reflected in one of his earlier  childhood observations where he argues  that a villain I think his name was a  crab demon was actually pretty amazing  for holding his own against so many  heroes but he's often something that  I've thought about because generally the  villains are much much more impressive  than the heroes which is why they all  need to team up to beat them me maybe  not so much in one punch man because  Saitama but in most other media although  initially I wasn't quite able to make  the leap from this idea to Cara's modern  day profession as a hero hunter but when  you realize that he quite purposely  doesn't kill them it does make a tinge  more sense and of course it's character  is made all the more complex by the fact  that despite calling himself a monster  he refuses to align himself with a more  traditional view of monsters it leaves  him in this wonderful gray area that  made him a really captivating presence  to watch on screen and of course all of  his action was fantastic and he was just  a real gem for this season in general  he's not the only one though because I  feel like the more extended casts get a  huge chance to shine here another  example of which being metal bat who  skyrocketed to being one of my favorite  characters in the entire series after I  saw his episodes he just exuded raw  amazingness and really left me with a  feeling of yes this is what an s-class  hero is which is very important in the  season where we are constantly  questioning the very idea of heroism  even our protagonist Saitama has to deal  with some degree where as usual his own  biggest challenge being himself I come  to think of it this season if one punch  man really did not see a whole lot of  Saitama his primary action was becoming  involved in the martial arts tournament  and yeah he swept in and one shot some  monsters here and there as per usual but  he wasn't at all a major focus which  oddly enough I think only helps the  series the real drama and action of one  punch man comes from people undergoing  legitimate struggles and there's only so  much you can do with Saitama struggle  with boredom so as much as I loved him I  didn't really miss him all that much  when he wasn't there although it was  still incredibly satisfying to see him  show up and wreak face every time he was  in a position to do so and I suppose I  should also mention the character of  sudio who was an interesting addition to  the world as I did very much enjoy  delving into the pursuit of strength  outside of the hero profession and  Sudhir provided some challenging ideas  but I really liked that when everything  was said and done even he was left  crying out for a hero to help him  finally realizing what the true purpose  of a hero is however despite these  fantastic characters I do think that  season two suffers from a narrative  perspective and yes I get that this is  going to happen because we're adapting a  manga here rather than specifically  catering a product for seasonal  television but in the end all that  really matters is the result for me and  it was a bit jarring at times one of the  better examples is the very first  episode which focuses heavily on the  character of  and his false heroics oh no now there  was nothing wrong with this episode on  its own it presents some great ideas and  it's entertaining but it's placed in an  awkward spot being the opener of the  season because I suppose that after  waiting what three or four years what I  wanted was something more Saitama or  Jenna's focused to bring me back into  this world and so kicking things off  with this in-depth exploration of King  just hit me as a bit off and once again  I get it that's probably how it happened  in the manga but from the perspective of  just watching the anime it's it's it is  what it is but expanding our gaze  overall the season felt very incomplete  there were two major plot lines  established being guru and the plans of  the monster Association and by the end  of the season neither were in any way  close to being resolved for the third  time once again I understand this is not  the fault of the anime as that was the  manga material they had to develop and  while I haven't read the manga my  understanding is that by the end of  season two we are essentially brought up  to date with it as it's currently  dealing with the two aforementioned  thoughts that's fine but it doesn't  really change the overall feeling of  this season not really having an ending  in fact it actually feels like the  halfway point of a season you know the  threat is established now and it's time  to kick into serious mode to deal with  it so it left me wanting more and not in  a good way like well that was a  satisfying series I certainly hope we  get another bit in more of a you just  ended the story halfway through and now  I don't know if I'll ever actually get  to see the end of it because it could  realistically be another three or four  years before we see a third outing if at  all so yeah it's next to impossible for  me to be satisfied with season 2 from a  narrative perspective but at the same  time like I said it was still highly  enjoyable because of the wonderful  characters of this world but now I guess  we should address the animation and  here's the thing even without comparing  j.c.staff to madhouse there were moments  where what the season provides is simply  lackluster for the most part the  animation was fine and even greatly  enjoyable but there are certain segments  of action that feel like they just  really didn't have that much put into  them for example in episode 2 when  Saitama is fighting foo buki there is a  point in which she sandwiches him in  concrete and the way in which he is  visually depicted to break out of it and  he is exceptionally underwhelming it's  just a basic flash of light and then  some slow-moving rock fragments with an  even slower moving entirely still image  of Saitama this moment is missing any  semblance of oomph and impact and it's  not the only time that the action feels  lacking either  there are moments like that scattered  throughout the entirety of these twelve  episodes that just make me want more  from what I'm watching and I have to  admit some of that may be because of how  funny I remember what madhouse did with  season 1 but for the most part I do  believe that these instances are just  plain and simple lesser quality  animation  and that's a shame but I guess that's  what we have luckily I don't feel like  these instances impact the overall  experience too much yes they are a bit  jarring but as a whole the animation  feels you know quite solid and in the  end I did thoroughly enjoy my time with  this second outing of one punch man the  only major detriment in my eyes was the  narrative itself but once again just  experiencing these characters once more  in this world makes up for it for me  anyway but that pretty much does it for  my final thoughts on season 2 of one  punch man if you enjoyed this video and  the content this channel produce in  general then please do consider donating  to the New World review patreon it could  support of all of your amazing people is  what continues to make this channel  possible and if patreon isn't quite your  style then please do leave this video  we'll action or subscribe because it  also helps spot this channel and  incredible amount and if you'd like to  join the fun and anytime then please do  head over to my discord server where a  wide array of shenanigan me takes place  on a daily basis and finally please do  comment with your own thoughts on one  punch man  season 2 this has been the new world  review and I'll see you next time  you  [Music]

